  "paras": [
   "For most of the twentieth century, the River Aire, which runs through a busy industrial city, was little more than an open sewer. Factories poured dyes and chemicals into the water, and by the 1980s almost nothing could live in it. People who grew up nearby still remember the smell, and few would have guessed that the river would ever be clean again.",
   "The change did not happen by accident. In the 1990s, the local government began to treat the water before letting it flow back into the river, and stricter laws forced factories to stop dumping waste. Volunteers joined in, clearing rubbish and planting trees along the banks. Little by little, the water grew clearer, and small fish began to return.",
   "The return of wildlife came next. Biologists who had once counted a handful of species now record more than forty kinds of fish, as well as otters, kingfishers and herons. For the city, the river has become a place to walk, cycle and relax. Property near the water is more valuable, and cafes along the bank are busy at weekends. In other words, a healthy river has turned out to be good business as well as good ecology.",
   "The story of the Aire is not unique, but it is not automatic either. Rivers recover only when pollution stops and people keep caring. As one campaigner put it, a river cannot clean itself while it is still being used as a dustbin. The lesson is simple: nature is patient, but only if we give it the chance."
  ],
